   "answer": "As the network scale (graph size $n$) expands, the sampled graph operator (or induced step-graphon) approaches the underlying latent graphon operator, and the discrepancy or sampling error between the graph and its underlying graphon shrinks [2605.29828_0011, 2605.29828_0008]. \n\nSpecifically, Theorem 4.3 demonstrates that the discrepancy $\\varepsilon_n$ (measured in the operator norm $\\lVert T_{W_{\\pi_u}(\\Delta)} - T_W \\rVert_{L^2 \\to L^2}$) is bounded by a term that decreases as $n$ grows:\n\n$$\\varepsilon_n \\leq CL \\left( \\sqrt{\\frac{\\log(2/\\delta)}{n}} + \\frac{1}{n} \\right)$$\n\nThis means that larger graphs serve as more faithful proxies of the underlying graphon, with the approximation error shrinking at a rate of $O_p\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{\\log n}{n}}\\right)$ [2605.29828_0008, 2605.29828_0007].",
